echanism of Brain Death potentials (SSEP) and tests to measure cerebral blood Brain injury has a number of causes, such as traumatic flow.4 or cerebrovascular injury and generalized hypoxia, all 2. Demonstration that cessation of these functions is of which produce brain edema. irreversible: Irreversibility is established by: Edema is accompanied by an increase in intracranial • Determination of the cause of loss of brain function. pressure leading to gradual decrease in cerebral • Exclusion of reversible conditions. circulation to the level of almost cessation, causing • Demonstration that the cessation of brain aseptic necrosis of the brain. Within 3-5 days, there functions persists for an appropriate period of occurs widespread brain destruction or pannecrosis observation. Box 8.1: Diagnostic clinical brain death criteria4,5","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prerequisites. Brain death is the absence of clinical brain function when the proximate cause is known and demonstrably irreversible. i. Clinical or neuroimaging evidence of an acute CNS catastrophe that is compatible with the clinical diagnosis of brain death. ii. Exclusion of complicating medical conditions that may confound clinical assessment. iii. No drug intoxication or poisoning. iv. Core temperature > 32°C (90°F)."},{"label":"B","text":"The three cardinal findings in brain death are coma, absence of brainstem reflexes and apnea.6"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8b0f4f3aec91","stem":"Absence of brainstem reflexes","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pupils i. Absent pupillary response to bright light. ii. Size: Mid position (4 mm) to dilated (9 mm)."},{"label":"B","text":"Ocular movement i. No oculocephalic reflex (Doll's eye phenomenon). ii. Absent oculovestibular reflex (Caloric test): No deviation of eyes to irrigation in each ear with 50 ml of cold water."},{"label":"C","text":"Facial sensation and facial motor response i. No corneal reflex to touch with a cotton swab. ii. No jaw reflex. iii. No grimacing to deep pressure on nail bed, supraorbital ridge, or temporomandibular joint."},{"label":"D","text":"Pharyngeal and tracheal reflexes i. No gag reflex: No response after stimulation of the posterior pharynx with tongue blade. ii. No cough response to bronchial suction."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-69dcc8fe3e13","stem":"Study of death in all its aspects is known as: 5. Brainstem dead are all, except: JIPMER 08 AIIMS 08 A. Weaned off from ventilator, no respiration for","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eugenics B. Thanatology 15 secs"},{"label":"C","text":"Dactylography D. Tricology B. Absent pupillary response"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topi